{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 New York Yankees burst out of the gate with one of the best starts in franchise history, going 15-6 in April and 19-9 in May to establish themselves as the class of the American League. Aaron Judge was the engine of it all, launching home runs at a historic pace and carrying a lineup that was deep, dangerous, and relentless. A memorable comeback in Cleveland on April 23rd — with Isiah Kiner-Falefa and Gleyber Torres delivering in the clutch — set the tone for what felt like a wire-to-wire championship run in the making. By June, the Yankees were an astounding 22-6, their best month in decades, and the city was buzzing with World Series dreams.\n\nThen came July, and with it, the first cracks. The Yankees went 13-13, a pedestrian record that masked deeper trouble — Wandy Peralta was getting torched in late-game situations, the bullpen that had been a strength began to wobble, and the offense occasionally went quiet when it mattered most. August was an outright disaster: a 10-18 record that stands as one of the most jarring collapses by a front-running team in recent memory. Clay Holmes, who had been near-unhittable in the first half, suddenly couldn't get anyone out. Aroldis Chapman imploded. The bullpen that had been the team's identity became its Achilles heel, and a double-digit lead in the division shrank to something manageable for rivals.\n\nSeptember brought a resurgence. The Yankees went 17-8, with Josh Donaldson's grand slam on August 17th serving as a spark, and Giancarlo Stanton delivering another grand slam on September 20th to remind everyone what this lineup was capable of. Young players like Oswaldo Cabrera stepped up, Aaron Judge completed one of the greatest individual seasons in baseball history by chasing and breaking Roger Maris's American League home run record with 62, and New York locked up the AL East with 99 wins. The machinery felt repaired, the rotation steadied, and the Yankees headed to October with genuine championship aspirations.\n\nThe postseason, however, proved to be a story of survival and then surrender. New York edged past Cleveland in the Division Series in a tense five-game battle, with Clarke Schmidt's implosion in Game 5 nearly ending the run before Harrison Bader and Giancarlo Stanton bailed the team out. But the Houston Astros awaited in the ALCS, and they were a different animal entirely — seasoned, disciplined, and deep. Alex Bregman and Chas McCormick punished New York's pitching staff, Yordan Alvarez was a force of nature, and the Yankees' bats went cold at the worst possible time. The dynasty-in-waiting finished where so many Yankees teams before them had: on the wrong side of October, left to wonder what might have been.",
